atus\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"A pwquality drop-in (/etc/security/pwquality.conf.d/10-complexity.conf) was created with minlen=12, and pam_pwquality.so is wired into both /etc/pam.d/system-auth and /etc/pam.d/password-auth (pre-existing PAM stack, confirmed present). Functional verification via pwscore showed an 11-character password rejected with the explicit message 'shorter than 12 characters', and this behavior persisted after a full reboot.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-b7d4d06a1f9447a6\",\"cmd-d5c99ff4dc2242b7\",\"cmd-ae353c21095a47cf\",\"cmd-725727ad45fc460b\"]},{\"id\":\"req-2\",\"requirement\":\"New user passwords on node1 must use at least three different character classes\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"minclass=3 was set in the same drop-in file. Functional verification confirmed a 15-char lowercase-only password and a 12-char two-class password were both rejected for 'less than 3 character classes' (the second failed on a dictionary check first but the minclass mechanism was independently demonstrated by the lowercase-only case), while a 12-char three-class random password (score 36) was accepted. Re-verified identically after reboot.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-b7d4d06a1f9447a6\",\"cmd-a0911c8c3a2f4ccf\",\"cmd-a1f75b29efbf458e\",\"cmd-725727ad45fc460b\"]},{\"id\":\"req-3\",\"requirement\":\"Configuration persists and enforcement is durable across a restart of node1\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"Pre-restart boot_id (d052eb57...) was captured alongside the config/PAM state; a backgrounded systemctl reboot returned an acknowledgement before the connection dropped; the connection then failed twice (timeout, reset) consistent with an actual reboot; a new boot_id (7a149124...) was observed afterward, matching the global after_executor snapshot; and the drop-in file, PAM wiring, and pwscore enforcement behavior (reject <12 chars, accept 3-class 12-char) were all re-confirmed post-reboot.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-0ec998a595e04182\",\"cmd-75433305dfc84250\",\"cmd-c9910acbcb414385\",\"cmd-e9ccfdc965fa46b6\",\"cmd-55ff181aeb8f4c30\",\"cmd-1995280cbe5146cf\",\"cmd-725727ad45fc460b\"]}],\"confidence\":0.9,\"operational_hygiene\":{\"score\":0.95,\"summary\":\"All state-changing commands map clearly to task purposes: creating the pwquality drop-in (final config), creating/deleting a throwaway test user to observe passwd -S state (bounded, fully reverted via cmd-e4993e9d64364ac0), and the backgrounded reboot required by the restart-evidence protocol. No configuration was left in a weakened, conflicting, or exposed state; failed-services and package-health checks were clean both before and after. The only residue is a small /tmp/reboot.log file and the expected post-reboot systemd-private directories, which are low-risk, incidental restart artifacts rather than unnecessary executor mutations.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-852e9bbd44174e98\",\"cmd-e4993e9d64364ac0\",\"cmd-75433305dfc84250\",\"global:after_executor:node1:failed-services\",\"global:after_executor:node1:package-health\",\"global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files\"]},\"overall_summary\":\"The task's password complexity requirements (minlen=12, minclass=3) were correctly implemented via a pwquality.conf.d drop-in on node1, with PAM wiring confirmed and enforcement functionally demonstrated through pwscore both before and after a genuine, well-evidenced reboot (distinct boot_ids, connection drop, recovered state). The independent node-restart evidence protocol is complete and internally consistent with the fixed lifecycle snapshots. Hygiene is clean: the one test artifact (throwaway user) was created and removed within the same trace, and reboot-related /tmp residue is incidental rather than an unnecessary mutation.\",\"limitations\":[\"Enforcement was verified via pwscore against the pwquality library directly rather than by attempting an actual `passwd` change for a real user through the PAM stack, though this is a standard and reasonable verification method given the same PAM module and config are used.\",\"The 12-char two-class test case (cmd-a0911c8c3a2f4ccf) was rejected by a dictionary check rather than directly by the minclass rule, so minclass enforcement for that specific case is inferred rather than directly isolated; the lowercase-only case in the same command more directly confirms minclass=3 behavior.\"]}","structured_output":{"requirements":[{"id":"req-1","requirement":"New user passwords on node1 must be at least 12 characters long","status":"satisfied","summary":"A pwquality drop-in (/etc/security/pwquality.conf.d/10-complexity.conf) was created with minlen=12, and pam_pwquality.so is wired into both /etc/pam.d/system-auth and /etc/pam.d/password-auth (pre-existing PAM stack, confirmed present). Functional verification via pwscore showed an 11-character password rejected with the explicit message 'shorter than 12 characters', and this behavior persisted after a full reboot.","evidence_ids":["cmd-b7d4d06a1f9447a6","cmd-d5c99ff4dc2242b7","cmd-ae353c21095a47cf","cmd-725727ad45fc460b"]},{"id":"req-2","requirement":"New user passwords on node1 must use at least three different character classes","status":"satisfied","summary":"minclass=3 was set in the same drop-in file. Functional verification confirmed a 15-char lowercase-only password and a 12-char two-class password were both rejected for 'less than 3 character classes' (the second failed on a dictionary check first but the minclass mechanism was independently demonstrated by the lowercase-only case), while a 12-char three-class random password (score 36) was accepted. Re-verified identically after reboot.","evidence_ids":["cmd-b7d4d06a1f9447a6","cmd-a0911c8c3a2f4ccf","cmd-a1f75b29efbf458e","cmd-725727ad45fc460b"]},{"id":"req-3","requirement":"Configuration persists and enforcement is durable across a restart of node1","status":"satisfied","summary":"Pre-restart boot_id (d052eb57...) was captured alongside the config/PAM state; a backgrounded syst